{
  "id": "Compound::DB01274 - Disease::DOID:3083",
  "verbalization": "Arformoterol, a member of the adrenergic beta-2 agonists class, is implicated in the treatment of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) through its ability to stimulate beta-2 receptors, which are predominantly found in airway smooth muscle. This mechanism is supported by the direct treatment relationship between Arformoterol and COPD, as well as its inclusion in the broader category of adrenergic beta-2 agonists. The shared ancestry with formoterol and salbutamol, both of which are also adrenergic beta-2 agonists, further reinforces the pharmacological link between Arformoterol and COPD, as these agents are known to relax airway smooth muscle and reduce inflammation.\n\nThe evidence is further strengthened by the fact that Arformoterol is associated with the class of compounds known as phenylethanolamines, which are involved in the regulation of bronchodilation. While the direct treatment relationship between Arformoterol and COPD is clear, the broader context of its classification within the adrenergic beta-2 agonist family suggests that its therapeutic effects are mediated through a common mechanism of action. This integration of pharmacological classification and clinical treatment outcomes provides a cohesive explanation for the potential reutilization of Arformoterol in the management of COPD.",
